  </style:page-layout><style:style style:name="frMath" style:family="graphic"><style:graphic-properties style:vertical-pos="middle" style:vertical-rel="text"/></style:style>
    <style:style style:name="Centered" style:family="paragraph" style:parent-style-name="Text_20_body"><style:paragraph-properties fo:text-align="center"/></style:style></office:automatic-styles>
  <office:master-styles><style:master-page style:name="Standard" style:page-layout-name="pm1"/></office:master-styles>
  <office:body><office:text><text:p text:style-name="Title">Exercise Sheet: Fictional Algebra</text:p><text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Each volunteer measured the weight of the fishing nets after each trip in a careful and orderly way. The harbour office summarised the growth of moss on the old stone walls over three consecutive weeks. The lighthouse crew mapped the growth of moss on the old stone walls in a careful and orderly way.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">1. Solve the equation using the quadratic formula:</text:p><text:p text:style-name="Centered"><draw:frame draw:style-name="frMath" draw:name="Quadratic" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="4.5cm" svg:height="1.6cm" draw:z-index="0"><draw:object><math:math display="block"><math:semantics><math:mrow><math:mi>x</math:mi><math:mo>=</math:mo><math:mfrac><math:mrow><math:mo>-</math:mo><math:mi>b</math:mi><math:mo>±</math:mo><math:msqrt><math:mrow><math:msup><math:mi>b</math:mi><math:mn>2</math:mn></math:msup><math:mo>-</math:mo><math:mn>4</math:mn><math:mi>a</math:mi><math:mi>c</math:mi></math:mrow></math:msqrt></math:mrow><math:mrow><math:mn>2</math:mn><math:mi>a</math:mi></math:mrow></math:mfrac></math:mrow><math:annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0">x = {-b +- sqrt{b^2 - 4 a c}} over {2 a}</math:annotation></math:semantics></math:math></draw:object></draw:frame></text:p><text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The survey team catalogued the amount of rain collected in copper gauges using only paper notebooks. The archive keeper mapped the colours of the evening sky over the dunes before the season ended.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">2. Check the closed form of the sum of squares:</text:p><text:p text:style-name="Centered"><draw:frame draw:style-name="frMath" draw:name="Sum" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="5.5cm" svg:height="1.8cm" draw:z-index="0"><draw:object><math:math display="block"><math:semantics><math:mrow><math:munderover><math:mo>∑</math:mo><math:mrow><math:mi>k</math:mi><math:mo>=</math:mo><math:mn>1</math:mn></math:mrow><math:mi>n</math:mi></math:munderover><math:msup><math:mi>k</math:mi><math:mn>2</math:mn></math:msup><math:mo>=</math:mo><math:mfrac><math:mrow><math:mi>n</math:mi><math:mo stretchy="false">(</math:mo><math:mi>n</math:mi><math:mo>+</math:mo><math:mn>1</math:mn><math:mo stretchy="false">)</math:mo><math:mo stretchy="false">(</math:mo><math:mn>2</math:mn><math:mi>n</math:mi><math:mo>+</math:mo><math:mn>1</math:mn><math:mo stretchy="false">)</math:mo></math:mrow><math:mn>6</math:mn></math:mfrac></math:mrow><math:annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0">sum from {k=1} to n k^2 = {n(n+1)(2n+1)} over 6</math:annotation></math:semantics></math:math></draw:object></draw:frame></text:p><text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Each volunteer recorded the songs sung at the autumn market using only paper notebooks. The survey team measured the colours of the evening sky over the dunes in a careful and orderly way. The harbour office summarised the colours of the evening sky over the dunes using only paper notebooks. Each volunteer summarised the tidal patterns of the northern bay before the season ended.</text:p></office:text></office:body>
</office:document>
